From: Davi <davilimacarv2536@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 22:38:41 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] compiler thread lifecycle

Gives the embedder control over when a `WTF::AutomaticThread` is born,
whether it may die, and what it does with its memory while it waits,
because a worker can rely on the defaults for none of the three. A
worker is forked from a zygote and then installs a seccomp filter
denying `clone`, so the set of threads alive when the filter goes on is
the set that exists forever: a thread that retires can never come back,
and a thread not yet created can never be created. Upstream gives
neither guarantee. An `AutomaticThread` self-terminates after an idle
timeout, and creates its OS thread lazily on the first notify that
carries work, which for a compiler thread means the first tier-up, long
after the filter. The patch adds a process-local idle-timeout override
so a thread can be pinned alive, and
`AutomaticThreadCondition::startAllThreads` so one can be started with
nothing to do: it polls once, gets `PollResult::Wait`, and parks. On
top of that sit `startThreads()` on the JS and wasm worklists and
`JSC::startCompilerThreads()` over both, so the worker brings up its
whole compiler topology in the window between the sandbox privilege
boundary and seccomp. Neither half is separable: the pin is pointless
if the thread was never started, and starting is pointless if it may
retire before the filter. Only a patch can do the starting, because
`AutomaticThread::start` is private to the class and its condition.
Handing a worklist trivial work purely so its thread appears as a side
effect (validating an empty wasm module) depends on that work still
reaching the queue, silently stops creating the thread the day it is
short-circuited, surfaces much later as an abort under load with
nothing pointing at the cause, and needs a different trick per
worklist. Starting a thread outside the normal wake path has one
obligation, and `JITWorklist::startThreads` discharges it: the worklist
counts threads that are awake, every wake pairs its notify with an
increment, and `JITWorklistThread::poll` asserts that count is non-zero
before decrementing it on the way to parking, so `startAllThreads`
returns how many it started and the caller credits them.

A pinned thread also has to do, while it waits, what retirement would
have done to its memory. Retiring ends the OS thread and with it the
thread's libpas cache, while a pinned thread keeps its allocators
active for the life of the process. libpas stops an idle thread's
allocators from the scavenger by suspending that thread with a signal,
which the worker's filter denies, so the embedder turns the suspender
off; the scavenger then finds those allocators active on every pass and
polls every period (100 ms on Linux) instead of reaching its deep sleep.
Only the owning thread can stop its own allocators.
`setColloAutomaticThreadCacheRelease` makes a thread that has waited
that long without work call `releaseFastMallocFreeMemoryForThisThread`
once, without the worklist's lock, and go back to waiting; the next
release comes only after the thread has worked again. The embedder
passes the interval after which libpas would have forced the stop
itself, `pas_local_allocator_should_stop_count_for_suspend` scavenger
periods. The wait now runs to a deadline measured from the start of the
idle period, so a parked thread woken by an override change measures
the time it has already waited against the new values; the old wait
retired a thread on any wake that was not a notify, which made a longer
timeout end the threads it was meant to keep.
`clearColloAutomaticThreadOverrides` removes both overrides.

The same patch also adds `JSC::drainCompilerWorklists`, which an
embedder about to fork needs for the mirror-image reason: a compiler
thread observes its idle timeout only while parked, so a plan still
compiling holds its thread against a caller waiting for the process to
become single-threaded. With the drain, tier-up before a fork is safe.
